oking fairly sunny all over the place: https://i.imgur.com/4LK7xo8.pngreplymatsemann 23 minutes ago | prev | next [–] https://www.timeanddate.com/eclipse/solar/2026-august-12Nice page showing where it will be, with times and visualizations for any point/city.replyMadrasTh0rn 17 minutes ago | prev | next [–] Very coolreply4gotunameagain 32 minutes ago | prev | next [–] Really cool. I'm quite curious as to where the webcam data came from, as it seems to be from various websites/sources.replymoron4hire 28 minutes ago | prev [–] I took my kids out of school and took an unplanned day off work in the middle of an important meeting series to drive 5 hours west from Virginia to Ohio to see the last eclipse. The schools and my boss were both yelling at me, both saying the eclipse wasn't a good enough excuse. I'm sorry, but it definitely was. I am so glad I did that. I would do it again in a heartbeat.replyUncle_Brumpus 7 minutes ago | parent [–] I drove ~3 hours to the Vermont-Canada border... and then drove 14 hours home. I'd also do it again in a heartbeat.I almost signed up for a live-work program for a big music festival happening in Iceland, but the flights (the only thing not covered) were astronomically expensive and I had already made plans with my dad last weekend, and he's getting older so I really didn't want to bail on him.I would love to sit atop the Rock of Gibraltar (with 47,000 other people) for the next one, Aug 2 2027, but that is likely also a pipe-dream, because NORMAL flights to Gibraltar are already too expensive for me.reply Guidelines | FAQ | Lists | API | Security | Legal | Apply to YC | Contact Search:\n=== END PAGE TEXT ===\n\nGoal: Visit Hacker News and:\n1. Identify the top story on the front page (the first article in the list)\n2. Drill into its comments thread\n3. Record the story title (key=story_title) and story URL (key=story_url) in working memory\n4. Record your summary of the top 3 comments as key=top_comments (one paragraph, 2-3 sentenc